  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x200b;&#x200b;Strengthening the Social Contract through Socio-Economic Infrastructure&#x200b;</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening the Social Contract through Socio Economic Infrastructure supports communities in newly liberated areas of Somalia to strengthen resilience and improve livelihoods. The project delivers area based, integrated support that combines climate adaptive socio economic infrastructure, sustainable natural resource management, and initiatives to strengthen trust between communities and local authorities. While security gains are important, long term stability depends on restoring basic services, strengthening local economies, and reducing environmental pressures that often drive tension and conflict. The project works with communities and local authorities to rehabilitate and construct small scale infrastructure such as markets, water systems, and community facilities. These investments are identified and delivered through participatory processes, with communities directly involved in planning and co financing, helping to ensure ownership and accountability. Alongside infrastructure, the project supports climate sensitive livelihoods by strengthening community based management of natural resources and encouraging investment in climate resilient value chains. Practical approaches such as soil and water conservation, regenerative agriculture, and shared resource use help reduce pressure on scarce resources while creating more sustainable income opportunities. With support from the European Union, the project also promotes inclusive local decision making, with particular attention to women&apos;s participation in mediation, natural resource management, and livelihoods, recognizing their role in peacebuilding and recovery.</narrative>
  </description>
